span class=3D"mord">&nbsp;S/m</span></span></span></span></span></span>), e=
lectrode topology dictates whether your signal can pierce through the bound=
ary layers to classify tissue composition or if it will simply short-circui=
t through the surrounding blood pool.</p><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"3">1. Top=
ography: Dimpled vs. Flat in a Flowing Vessel</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"4=
">In intravascular electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), the choice betw=
een a flat and a textured surface focuses heavily on fluid dynamics, bounda=
ry layers, and tissue compliance.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"5">Dimpled El=
ectrodes</h3><ul data-path-to-node=3D"6"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"6,0,0"=
><b data-path-to-node=3D"6,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Boundary Layer Dis=
ruption:</b> As blood flows rapidly over a catheter tip, a hydrodynamic bou=
ndary layer forms. A dimpled surface introduces localized micro-turbulence =
that disrupts this high-impedance layer, ensuring fresh, representative blo=
od elements directly interact with the electrode surface.</p></li><li><p da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"6,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"6,1,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"0">Exclusion of Plasma Skimming:</b> During contact with a pulmonary ar=
tery wall or an organizing thrombus, dimples act as miniature traps for red=
 blood cell aggregation or fibrin networks. This heightens sensitivity to t=
he distinct capacitive behavior of a clot (<span class=3D"math-inline" data=
-math=3D"\Delta \varepsilon_r" data-index-in-node=3D"257"><span class=3D"ka=
tex"><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><=
