-path-to-node=3D"8"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"8,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Hemodynamic Neutrality:</b> Flat (or fl=
ush-mounted ring/band) electrodes maintain a laminar flow profile along the=
 catheter shaft, preventing localized clotting risk and reducing shear-stre=
ss artifacts on blood cells.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0"><b =
data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Predictable Electric F=
ield Penetration:</b> When modeling the multi-frequency impedance of a vess=
el wall versus a clot, flat electrodes provide clean, parallel-ish or well-=
mapped fringe field lines. This simplifies the inverse problem algorithms u=
sed to convert complex impedance parameters into tissue classification.</p>=
</li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"8,2,0" data=
-index-in-node=3D"0">Susceptibility to Blood "Shunting":</b> If a flat elec=
trode is placed near a clot but not firmly pressed against it, the highly c=
onductive surrounding blood acts as an electrical short circuit (shunt), co=
mpletely masking the signature of the underlying thrombus or vessel wall.</=
p></li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"10">2. Curvature: Convex vs. Concave o=
n a Catheter Profile</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"11">Because the target env=
ironment consists of a cylindrical tube (the artery), a semi-solid mass (th=
e clot), and a fluid (blood), the macro-geometry of the electrode determine=
s how well it physically interfaces with these media.</p><h3 data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"12">Convex Electrodes (Domed / Protruding Buttons)</h3><ul data-path=
-to-node=3D"13"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"13,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D=
"13,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Wall &amp; Clot Indentation:</b> Convex e=
lectrodes (e.g., small micro-domes protruding slightly from the catheter ti=
p or side-wall) are ideal for <b data-path-to-node=3D"13,0,0" data-index-in=
-node=3D"138">mechanical displacement</b>. When the catheter navigates an e=
mbolism or brushes the vessel wall, the convex surface physically pushes pa=
st the ambient blood film, forcing direct contact with the solid tissue mat=
rix.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"13,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"13=
,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">High-Density Focus:</b> Current density spik=
es at the apex of a convex electrode. This provides a highly localized "pro=
be" behavior, giving you excellent spatial resolution to pinpoint the exact=
 boundary where a clot terminates and the true lumen begins.</p></li></ul><=
h3 data-path-to-node=3D"14">Concave Electrodes (Recessed Bands / Wells)</h3=
><ul data-path-to-node=3D"15"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"15,0,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"15,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Blood-Only Reference Sensor=
s:</b> If electrodes are recessed into the catheter body (forming a concave=
 well), they are shielded from direct contact with the vessel wall or a clo=
t.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"15,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"15,1=
,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Differential Sensing Strategy:</b> This can be=
 highly advantageous when used as a <i data-path-to-node=3D"15,1,0" data-in=
dex-in-node=3D"78">baseline reference</i>. By tracking the pure blood imped=
ance in a recessed concave pocket, you can continuously calibrate your syst=
em's ambient conductivity and temperature baseline, making it easier for yo=
ur forward-facing or convex electrodes to isolate the delta caused by a thr=
ombus.</p></li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"17">3. Geometric Shape: Circul=
ar Disks vs. Rectangular Bands</h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"18">The geometri=
c profile dictates the orientation of your sensing field relative to the ca=
theter's axis of travel.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"19">Circular (or Oval)=
 Micro-Electrodes</h3><ul data-path-to-node=3D"20"><li><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"20,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"20,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Spot C=
haracterization:</b> Arranging tiny circular electrodes in a radial or forw=
ard-facing array allows for point-source impedance spectroscopy. This shape=
 maximizes spatial resolution, letting you detect if a clot is only partial=
ly occluding one side of the pulmonary artery.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-=
node=3D"20,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"20,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Is=
otropic Signature:</b> Circular geometry means the contact force and orient=
