pan></span></span></span>) of the underlying thrombus or wall tissue.</p></=
li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"8">2. Fluid Dynamics and Boundary Layers</=
h2><p data-path-to-node=3D"9">The blood film is not completely static, but =
it is heavily influenced by fluid mechanics. As the catheter moves through =
the pulmonary artery, fluid dynamics dictate two distinct layers at the ele=
ctrode interface:</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"10"><li><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"10,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Hy=
drodynamic Boundary Layer:</b> Right at the solid surface of a flat electro=
de, blood velocity drops to zero (the no-slip condition). This creates a st=
agnant plasma/blood layer that acts as a buffer.</p></li><li><p data-path-t=
o-node=3D"10,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">=
Plasma Skimming:</b> Near the vessel wall or around a tight obstruction lik=
e a clot, red blood cells tend to migrate toward the center of high-velocit=
y flow, leaving a cell-depleted, plasma-rich layer right against the wall o=
r catheter surface. Because plasma lacks the capacitive cell membranes of r=
ed blood cells, this thin film exhibits almost purely resistive behavior, s=
tripping away the multi-frequency capacitive signatures you need to detect =
structural differences in a clot.</p></li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=3D"12"=
>3. Breaking Through the Film: Mechanics vs. Frequency</h2><p data-path-to-=
node=3D"13">To successfully detect an embolism despite this film, the cathe=
ter design must either mechanically displace the blood layer or use electri=
cal frequencies capable of bypassing it.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"14">Me=
chanical Displacement (Topology)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"15">This is wh=
ere <b data-path-to-node=3D"15" data-index-in-node=3D"14">convex (domed) ci=
rcular electrodes</b> become critical.</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"16"><li>=
<p data-path-to-node=3D"16,0,0">As the catheter face presses against a clot=
, the outward protrusion of a convex electrode concentrates the physical fo=
rce onto a microscopic apex.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"16,1,0">Th=
is localized high pressure physically squeezes the highly conductive blood =
film out of the interface, forcing direct, intimate contact with the struct=
ural fibrin-platelet matrix of the clot or the cellular layers of the vesse=
l wall.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"17">Frequency-Dependent Penet=
ration (Electrical Breakdown)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"18">If a flat ele=
ctrode is used and a micro-film of blood persists, the choice of measuremen=
t frequency determines if you can see past it:</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"=
19"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"19,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"19,0,0" dat=
a-index-in-node=3D"0">At Low Frequencies (&lt; 10 kHz):</b> The current can=
not easily cross the capacitive cell membranes within the blood or the elec=
trode polarization layer. It stays entirely trapped in the fluid film, meas=
uring only the plasma shunt.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"19,1,0"><b=
 data-path-to-node=3D"19,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">At High Frequencies =
(&gt; 100 kHz to 1 MHz):</b> The current can capacitively couple <i data-pa=
th-to-node=3D"19,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"78">across</i> the thin blood f=
ilm and pierce through the cellular membranes of both the blood cells and t=
he target tissue. This allows the system to read the bulk intrinsic impedan=
ce of the clot or wall behind the film.</p></li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=
=3D"21">Implications for Signal Classification Algorithms</h2><p data-path-=
to-node=3D"22">If your catheter system relies on a predictive algorithm (li=
ke a machine learning model or a threshold classifier) to differentiate bet=
ween blood, clot, and wall, the presence of an unstable blood film introduc=
es a gradient of contact rather than a binary "yes/no" contact state.</p><p=
