ions requires high-resolution differentiation between circulating blood, em=
bolic clots, and the vessel wall. This paper outlines the physical and elec=
trochemical requirements for an Electrical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) sen=
sor. We demonstrate that a tetrapolar (4-electrode) configuration utilizing=
 convex circular electrodes at 50 kHz provides the optimal balance between =
signal-to-noise ratio, mechanical displacement of the conductive blood film=
, and rejection of electrode polarization artifacts.</p><p data-path-to-nod=
