&lt;body&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"header-container"&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;Optimization of Electrode Geometry and Tetrapolar Configu=
rations for High-Fidelity Detection of Thrombi in the Pulmonary Artery&lt;/=
h1&gt;
        &lt;div class=3D"subtitle"&gt;A Comprehensive Electrophysical and E=
lectrochemical Analysis for Intravascular Impedance Spectroscopy&lt;/div&gt=
;
        &lt;div class=3D"author-block"&gt;
            &lt;strong&gt;Advanced Sensor Systems Division&lt;/strong&gt;&l=
t;br&gt;
            Technical White Paper | June 2026
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"abstract-box"&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Abstract&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;The real-time detection and characterization of acute pulm=
onary artery embolisms (PE) within fluidic endovascular domains require pre=
cise electromagnetic tissue classification. Traditional intravascular monit=
oring is fundamentally limited by the shunt impedance of surrounding blood =
fractions. This technical white paper examines the electrophysical and elec=
trochemical optimization of an endovascular catheter-based sensor array. We=
 provide formal mathematical frameworks governing the Electrical Double Lay=
er (EDL), Electrode Polarization (EP), the tissue-specific dispersion bound=
aries, and fluidic boundary shunts. Through parametric analysis, we demonst=
rate that a tetrapolar (four-electrode) array using localized convex, circu=
lar electrode topologies operated at an optimized frequency window of 50 kH=
z achieves complete rejection of interface polarization artifacts and deliv=
ers high-fidelity tissue identification based heavily on complex phase angl=
e vector stabilization.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;1. Introduction &amp; The Biophysical Environment&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Mechanical thrombectomy and endovascular evaluation of pulmona=
ry artery occlusions present severe diagnostic challenges due to the highly=
 dynamic fluid environment of human blood. Circulating blood operates as an=
 excellent electrical conductor, exhibiting a bulk conductivity (&lt;span c=
lass=3D"math"&gt;&amp;sigma;&lt;sub&gt;b&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;) of appro=
ximately &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;0.7 &amp;plusmn; 0.05 &amp;nbsp; S/m&lt=
;/span&gt; at normal core body temperature (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;37&a=
mp;deg;C&lt;/span&gt;). This high conductivity is mediated primarily by fre=
e-floating electrolytic fractions (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Na&lt;sup&gt;=
+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Cl&lt;sup&gt;&amp;mi=
nus;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;HCO&lt;sub&gt;3&l=
t;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;) within the blood=
 plasma.&lt;/p&gt;
   =20
    &lt;p&gt;Conversely, an organized embolic mass (thrombus) consists of a=
 dense, cross-linked structural matrix of polymerized fibrin strands, activ=
ated platelet aggregates, and trapped, decompressing red blood cells (eryth=
rocytes). This composition results in a significantly lower bulk conductivi=
ty (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;sigma;&lt;sub&gt;c&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;asy=
mp; 0.15&amp;nbsp;--&amp;nbsp;0.25 &amp;nbsp; S/m&lt;/span&gt;). When an im=
pedance-sensing catheter navigates this boundary, the physical presence of =
the highly conductive plasma creates a massive parallel pathway, or electri=
cal "shunt," surrounding the electrode elements. Current path allocation au=
tomatically favors the lower-impedance plasma medium, effectively short-cir=
cuiting the underlying target structure and yielding profound measurement e=
rrors in conventional bipolar sensors.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;2. Mathematical Formulations of the Interface&lt;/h2&gt;
   =20
    &lt;h3&gt;2.1 The Electrical Double Layer (EDL) &amp; Constant Phase El=
ement&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;When a metallic electrode (such as platinum-iridium or gold) i=
nterfaces with blood, a spontaneous charging mechanism initiates to establi=
sh thermodynamic and electrochemical equilibrium. This forms the Electrical=
 Double Layer (EDL), structurally categorized by the Stern-Grahame model in=
to the Inner Helmholtz Plane (IHP), Outer Helmholtz Plane (OHP), and a diff=
use thermal transport region (Gouy-Chapman layer). Due to microscopic surfa=
ce irregularities, molecular adsorption of plasma proteins (e.g., albumin, =
fibrinogen), and spatial non-homogeneities, the electrical behavior cannot =
be characterized as an ideal pure capacitor. Instead, it must be mathematic=
ally modeled as a Constant Phase Element (CPE), whose complex impedance is =
expressed by the following relation:&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"equation-block"&gt;
        &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;CPE&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;)=
 =3D &amp;nbsp; &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;frasl; &lt;sub&gt;[ Q&lt;sub&=
gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;middot; ( j &amp;omega; )&lt;sup&gt;&amp;alpha;&lt;/s=
up&gt; ]&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Where &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Q&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;=
/span&gt; denotes the numerical pseudo-capacitance coefficient (proportiona=
l to the absolute double-layer capacitance &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;C&lt;=
sub&gt;dl&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, which typically ranges from &lt;span cl=
ass=3D"math"&gt;10&amp;nbsp;--&amp;nbsp;40 &amp;nbsp; &amp;mu;F/cm&lt;sup&g=
t;2&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;), &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;j&lt;/span&gt; is=
 the imaginary unit (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;radic;&amp;minus;1&lt;=
/span&gt;), &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;omega; =3D 2&amp;pi;f&lt;/span&=
gt; is the angular frequency, and &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;alpha;&lt=
;/span&gt; is an empirical structural attenuation factor bounded between &l=
t;span class=3D"math"&gt;0 &amp;le; &amp;alpha; &amp;le; 1&lt;/span&gt;. Fo=
r a highly polished, atomistically flat surface, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt=
;&amp;alpha; &amp;to; 1&lt;/span&gt;. For highly textured, dimpled, or biol=
ogically fouled surfaces, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;alpha;&lt;/span&g=
t; drops toward &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;0.8&lt;/span&gt;, reflecting a d=
istributed spectrum of dielectric relaxation frequencies across the interfa=
ce.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h3&gt;2.2 Total Measured Two-Electrode Impedance&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;In a standard two-electrode (bipolar) circuit configuration, t=
he same pair of electrodes injects the excitation current and senses the se=
condary voltage drop. The total measured impedance vector (&lt;span class=
=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;total&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;) is the simple seri=
es combination of the interface boundaries and the bulk target volume:&lt;/=
p&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"equation-block"&gt;
        &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;total&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega=
;) =3D 2 &amp;middot; Z&lt;sub&gt;CPE&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;) + Z&lt;sub&g=
t;bulk&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Where &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;bulk&lt;/sub&gt;&=
lt;/span&gt; encompasses the combined resistance and reactance of the tissu=
e volume (blood, clot, or arterial wall) swept by the electric field lines.=
 At low frequencies, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;CPE&lt;/sub&gt;=
 &amp;raquo; Z&lt;sub&gt;bulk&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, meaning that over &=
lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;90\%&lt;/span&gt; of the total voltage drop occur=
s across a sub-nanometer fluid boundary layer rather than the physical tiss=
ue matrix, rendering data extraction mathematically unstable.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;3. Geometric Optimization and Topology&lt;/h2&gt;

    &lt;h3&gt;3.1 Dimpled vs. Flat Topographies&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Surface micro-structuring presents distinct mechanical and ele=
ctrical trade-offs. The total double-layer capacitance is directly proporti=
onal to the true microscopic surface area (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;A&lt;=
sub&gt;micro&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;):&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"equation-block"&gt;
        &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;C&lt;sub&gt;dl&lt;/sub&gt; =3D &amp;nbsp=
; &lt;sup&gt;(&amp;epsilon;&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;epsilon;&lt;sub&gt=
;r&lt;/sub&gt; A&lt;sub&gt;micro&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;frasl; &lt;s=
ub&gt;d&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Where &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;epsilon;&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;=
/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt; is vacuum permittivity, &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&a=
mp;epsilon;&lt;sub&gt;r&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt; is the relative permittivi=
ty of the OHP layer, and &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt; is the c=
ompact double-layer thickness. Introducing precise microscopic dimples or t=
exturing dramatically scales &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;A&lt;sub&gt;micro&l=
t;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt; without increasing the nominal geometric footprint =
of the catheter sensor. This surge in area inflates &lt;span class=3D"math"=
&gt;Q&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, thereby shifting the corner fre=
quency of electrode polarization downward. However, inside high-flow pulmon=
ary arteries, surface dimples introduce localized micro-stagnation zones, s=
everely increasing the long-term risk of catheter-induced thrombosis via lo=
calized platelet aggregation.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h3&gt;3.2 Curvature: The Convex Mechanical Displacement Advantage&l=
t;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;To eliminate the fluid-film shunt, the electrode must alter th=
e immediate physical boundary. A flat electrode configuration allows the hy=
drodynamic boundary layer and cell-free plasma skimming layers to remain un=
disturbed, trapping a highly conductive film between the sensor and the clo=
t. Alternatively, a convex (domed) geometry acts as a mechanical stress con=
centrator.&lt;/p&gt;
   =20
    &lt;p&gt;As the catheter advances into an embolic mass, the outward mec=
hanical force creates a high localized pressure point at the apex of the do=
me. This physical force expresses the low-viscosity blood film completely o=
ut of the interface zone, forcing direct contact with the solid fibrin-plat=
elet mesh of the thrombus. Furthermore, current density (&lt;span class=3D"=
math"&gt;J&lt;/span&gt;) scales exponentially with curvature sharpness, for=
cing maximum current flux to exit the apex, projecting the electrical inter=
rogation field deep into the target tissue core.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h3&gt;3.3 Shape: Circular Symmetric Profiling&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Rectangular or strip electrodes introduce extensive analytical=
 errors in endovascular applications. Sharp boundaries and long straight ma=
rgins produce catastrophic "current crowding" effects, where electric field=
 lines intensely concentrate at the corners. This produces non-uniform loca=
l current densities, distorting the nominal &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp=
;alpha;&lt;/span&gt; factor of the interface CPE. Circular geometries guara=
ntee perfect radial symmetry. This ensures the output current field is isot=
ropic, meaning the measured complex impedance profile is completely indepen=
dent of the rotational or twisting orientation of the catheter within the p=
ulmonary artery branches.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;4. Frequency Optimization &amp; Biological Dispersion&lt;/h2&=
gt;
    &lt;p&gt;The system utilizes a driving voltage threshold of &lt;span cl=
ass=3D"math"&gt;&amp;plusmn;1.5 &amp;nbsp; V&lt;/span&gt;, establishing a &=
lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;3.0 &amp;nbsp; V&lt;/span&gt; peak-to-peak differ=
ential window across the current injection pair. The thermodynamic potentia=
l for water splitting and electrolysis in an aqueous saline solution is &lt=
;span class=3D"math"&gt;1.23 &amp;nbsp; V&lt;/span&gt;. Driving a DC or low=
-frequency signal at &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;3.0 &amp;nbsp; V&lt;/span&g=
t; causes immediate faradaic electron transfers, liberating hydrogen and ox=
ygen gas bubbles that act as electrical insulators and pose high air-emboli=
sm risks to the patient.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Operating at an optimized frequency of &lt;b&gt;50 kHz&lt;/b&g=
t; provides an ideal electrochemical and electrophysical window. The voltag=
e switches polarity every &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;10 &amp;nbsp; &amp;mu;=
s&lt;/span&gt;, a timeline far too brief for hydrated ions to establish the=
 steady-state diffusion gradients required for faradaic phase transitions. =
Thus, the interface behaves in a purely non-faradaic, capacitive charging m=
ode, ensuring complete patient safety and bubble suppression.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;Physically, 50 kHz sits at the center of the biological &lt;b&=
gt;&amp;beta;-dispersion spectrum&lt;/b&gt;. At this frequency, the current=
 transitions from flowing purely around extracellular paths to capacitively=
 coupling across cellular membranes via Maxwell-Wagner structural relaxatio=
n. This enables distinct tissue classification based on individual cellular=
 matrices:&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;table&gt;
        &lt;thead&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Target Medium&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Conductivity (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;sig=
ma;&lt;/span&gt;, S/m)&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Permittivity (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;eps=
ilon;&lt;sub&gt;r&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt; at 50 kHz)&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Phase Angle (&lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;thet=
a;&lt;/span&gt; at 50 kHz)&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;th&gt;Primary Physical Mechanism&lt;/th&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/thead&gt;
        &lt;tbody&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Circulating Blood&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;0.72&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;~ 2,500&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;&amp;minus;5&amp;deg; to &amp;minus;12&amp;deg;&l=
t;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Current channels freely through conductive extrac=
ellular plasma fluid.&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Embolic Clot&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;0.21&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;~ 18,000&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;&amp;minus;35&amp;deg; to &amp;minus;52&amp;deg;&=
lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Dense, stagnant cross-linked fibrin grid acts as =
a massive capacitive array.&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
            &lt;tr&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Pulmonary Wall&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;0.38&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;~ 7,000&lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;&amp;minus;20&amp;deg; to &amp;minus;32&amp;deg;&=
lt;/td&gt;
                &lt;td&gt;Highly structured, anchored endothelial and smoot=
h muscle layers.&lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;5. Bipolar (2-Electrode) vs. Tetrapolar (4-Electrode) Archite=
cture&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;While geometric optimization significantly improves contact me=
chanics, a bipolar system is fundamentally incapable of isolating the subtl=
e phase variances of an organizing thrombus under dynamic movement due to t=
he overwhelming presence of &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt;CPE&lt;/=
sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;. Upgrading to a tetrapolar configuration fully decoupl=
es the excitation current loop from the potential sensing loop.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;An outer pair of convex, circular electrodes (&lt;span class=
=3D"math"&gt;I&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class=3D"m=
ath"&gt;I&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;) injects the fixed=
 50 kHz current. An inner pair of smaller circular, flat electrodes (&lt;sp=
an class=3D"math"&gt;V&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span cl=
ass=3D"math"&gt;V&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;) is wired =
directly to a high-impedance instrumentation amplifier with an input resist=
ance exceeding &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;1 &amp;nbsp; G&amp;Omega;&lt;/spa=
n&gt;. Because the current passing through the measurement line approaches =
