&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;The tetrapolar configuration strips away the shifting contact =
resistance and interface noise. The instrumentation amplifier captures only=
 the unperturbed electrical potential gradient of the bulk tissue volume. T=
his permits pristine evaluation of the true &lt;b&gt;Phase Angle (&lt;span =
class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;theta;&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/b&gt;, which serves as a ge=
ometry-independent structural fingerprint. Because phase angle depends sole=
ly on the ratio of tissue capacitive reactance to tissue resistance (&lt;sp=
an class=3D"math"&gt;&amp;theta; =3D \arctan(X_c / R)&lt;/span&gt;), it rem=
ains remarkably invariant to sudden contact area fluctuations caused by car=
diac pulsations.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;6. Differential Ambient Subtraction &amp; COMSOL Validation&l=
t;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;To ensure high sensitivity across varying patient demographics=
, the system couples the active tetrapolar tip array with a separate, ident=
ical reference channel located further back on the catheter shaft. This ref=
erence channel is physically recessed inside a shallow micro-well, shieldin=
g it from wall or clot contact while ensuring continuous contact with free-=
flowing blood. The software continuously calculates a Normalized Impedance =
Index (NII) vectorially:&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;div class=3D"equation-block"&gt;
        &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;NII(&amp;omega;) =3D &amp;nbsp; &lt;sup&=
gt;[ Z&lt;sub&gt;active&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;) &amp;minus; Z&lt;sub&gt;re=
f&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;) ]&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;frasl; &lt;sub&gt;Z&lt;sub&gt=
;ref&lt;/sub&gt;(&amp;omega;)&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;By assessing the real-time scalar and imaginary vector deviati=
ons away from the pure blood reference, the algorithm completely filters ou=
t systemic background noise such as hematocrit fluctuations, heart-rate ind=
uced shear orientation shifts, and core temperature drifts.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;3D Finite Element Analysis (FEA) completed in COMSOL Multiphys=
ics using the AC/DC Electric Currents module explicitly proves this operati=
onal mechanism. Parametric sweeps varying an artificial blood-film shunt la=
yer from &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;0&amp;nbsp;--&amp;nbsp;200 &amp;nbsp; &=
amp;mu;m&lt;/span&gt; demonstrate that in a 2-electrode simulation, the pha=
se angle signal crashes completely back to the blood baseline within the fi=
rst &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;45 &amp;nbsp; &amp;mu;m&lt;/span&gt; of flui=
d separation. In contrast, the tetrapolar convex configuration preserves ov=
er &lt;span class=3D"math"&gt;88\%&lt;/span&gt; of the target clot's comple=
x phase signature across the entire sweep range, confirming the extreme des=
ign robustness of the multi-electrode system.&lt;/p&gt;

